# This service does not auto-load .env: export these values through systemd,
# Docker or the invoking shell. OPC UA TCP port. Default: 4840.
PORT=4840

# Real username/password for the one authenticated role this server
# defines (write access to MaintenanceMode - see src/server.ts). Both
# unset (the default) means no username/password login is possible at
# all - isValidUser rejects every credential rather than shipping a
# silent default login. Every other client stays anonymous/read-only.
# OPCUA_ADMIN_USERNAME=hydra-admin
# OPCUA_ADMIN_PASSWORD=change-me

# Every real session requires Sign or SignAndEncrypt with a real,
# current security policy (Basic256Sha256, Aes128_Sha256_RsaOaep, or
# Aes256_Sha256_RsaPss) by default - a real SecurityPolicy.None
# connection attempt is genuinely refused, not silently accepted (see
# src/server.ts and tests/security-policy.test.ts). Setting this to the
# exact string "1" re-enables an UNENCRYPTED SecurityPolicy.None
# endpoint too, for a quick local-dev smoke test against a client that
# cannot yet manage a certificate exchange - never set this in a real
# deployment.
# OPCUA_ALLOW_INSECURE=1

# Robot roster source. When HYDRA_SERVER_URL is set together with a
# credential, the robot roster (controllers[].robots in GET /api/settings of
# HYDRA-UMC-SERVER) is read with it and one Robot_<id> object per robot is
# kept in the address space. If the source cannot be read the robots stay
# listed but are shown offline. Polling interval in milliseconds (minimum
# 1000, default 5000).
# The credential is a bearer token the server already issued (it expires with
# the session), or an account the poller logs in with itself and logs in with
# again when the session is refused - the better choice for a long-running
# service. When both are set the token is used first and the account renews it.
# HYDRA_SERVER_URL=http://127.0.0.1:3000
# HYDRA_SERVER_TOKEN=paste-a-session-token
# HYDRA_SERVER_USER=operator
# HYDRA_SERVER_PASSWORD=change-me
# HYDRA_SERVER_POLL_MS=5000

# Certificates. An unknown client application certificate waits in
# <OPCUA_PKI_DIR>/PKI/rejected until you move it to PKI/trusted/certs;
# OPCUA_AUTO_ACCEPT=1 accepts every client (a lab setting). User certificates
# (UserPKI) are never auto-accepted. Keep OPCUA_PKI_DIR on a persistent volume,
# or every restart presents a new certificate to your clients.
# OPCUA_PKI_DIR=/data/pki
# OPCUA_AUTO_ACCEPT=1

# The host name the server certificate and the advertised endpoints use, when
# it differs from the machine's own (for example a container id).
# OPCUA_ALTERNATE_HOSTNAME=opcua.plant.example

# Failed logins allowed per minute before logins are refused until it passes.
# OPCUA_LOGIN_MAX_FAILURES=10
